{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is a professional headshot generation system?

A professional headshot generation system turns a small set of consented photos into a
business-ready portrait. The generator is only one part of the system. The product must
verify who may use the photos, preserve the subject's identity, apply a bounded visual
style, inspect the result, and protect both the source and generated images.

The core invariant is **authorized likeness with faithful identity**: release an image
only when the requester is authorized to use the subject's likeness, the result remains
recognizably that subject, and the image passes the selected professional and safety
policy. This walkthrough uses an interview baseline of **50,000 final headshots per
day**, a **60-second p95 completion target**, and separate interactive and enterprise
batch queues.

- **Prerequisite:** Review [text-to-image generation](/practice/text-to-image) if
  diffusion, conditioning, or asynchronous GPU jobs are unfamiliar.
- **First-version boundary:** Generate still portraits from user-provided photos. Do not
  offer public-figure cloning, unverified third-party likeness editing, or an open-ended
  photo editor.
{% /section-card %}

{% accordion defaultOpen="clarifying" %}

{% accordion-item id="clarifying" title="1. Clarify the use case and guardrails" %}


### Start with a narrow, observable user journey

1. A user uploads 3-10 photos of themself, accepts consent and retention terms, and
   chooses a professional preset.
2. The service checks face count, image quality, ownership signals, and policy before
   consuming GPU capacity.
3. It creates a low-cost preview set, then makes an approved final set available after
   independent identity, composition, and policy checks.
4. The user downloads a selected result; an enterprise admin may apply a reviewed brand
   preset and audit the job.

### Requirements that change the system

{% concept-grid columns="2" %}
{% concept-card title="Functional scope" eyebrow="Product" icon="users" tone="blue" %}
- Consent-backed selfie uploads and deletion requests.
- Bounded controls for attire category, background, lighting, and crop.
- Preview, rerun, selection, and export workflows.
- Enterprise brand presets with tenant-scoped review and audit evidence.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Service promises" eyebrow="SLO" icon="gauge" tone="green" %}
- Return job acceptance immediately and complete standard jobs within 60 seconds at p95.
- Deliver a 2048 px final image after all release gates pass.
- Keep an accepted job durable across worker loss; make retry and cancellation idempotent.
- Keep source photos and output assets isolated by tenant and retention policy.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Safety boundaries" eyebrow="Likeness" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
- Require a single consenting subject in accepted reference photos.
- Block public-figure, deceptive impersonation, and disallowed image-edit requests.
- Do not make a person materially younger, change protected traits, or fabricate credentials.
- Escalate uncertain identity, consent, or policy evidence rather than generating.
{% /concept-card %}

{% concept-card title="Explicit exclusions" eyebrow="Scope" icon="boxes" tone="neutral" %}
- No real-time video or live camera stream.
- No automatic HR approval or identity verification beyond the stated product evidence.
- No guarantee that every casual photo is recoverable into a professional portrait.
- No reuse of a tenant's brand preset for another tenant.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% callout variant="tip" %}
The system's best degraded response is to request clearer, single-subject inputs or
offer a safe retry. It must not invent facial details, silently weaken identity checks,
or bypass policy to make a job look successful.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="calculations" title="2. Estimate token, media, and accelerator capacity" %}


### Convert requests into work

Assume 50,000 final headshots per day, 2.5 preview candidates per final image, a 3x
peak multiplier, and a 25% headroom reserve. A standard final render takes 45 seconds
of accelerator time; previews use a smaller 10-second pass. A batch should contain
only jobs with the same model tier and resolution so latency is measurable.

{% metric-strip %}
{% metric value="50K/day" label="Final images" detail="About 0.58 final images/second on average." tone="blue" /%}
{% metric value="125K/day" label="Preview candidates" detail="Direction finding consumes work too; account for it explicitly." tone="violet" /%}
{% metric value="3x" label="Peak multiplier" detail="Capacity must survive bursty business-hour demand, not only daily average." tone="amber" /%}
{% metric value="25%" label="Headroom" detail="Reserved for retries, worker loss, and placement fragmentation." tone="green" /%}
{% /metric-strip %}

### Test a capacity plan

{% interactive-block id="practice/headshot-generation-capacity-lab" /%}

The exact accelerator count depends on model, resolution, batch size, and benchmarked
p95 throughput. The useful interview calculation is the chain: demand becomes preview
and final jobs, jobs become accelerator seconds, and peak work plus headroom becomes a
fleet target. Keep queue age, not just GPU utilization, as the admission-control signal.

### Budget media separately from model work

- **Inputs:** 8 uploaded photos at 5 MB on average is roughly 2 TB/day before retries
  and replication at this scale. Store encrypted originals in object storage, not in a
  job database.
- **Outputs:** 4 final images at 8 MB each is roughly 1.6 TB/day before derivatives;
  previews can be lower resolution and shorter retention.
- **Metadata:** job stage, model version, consent record, policy version, scores, and
  asset IDs fit in a transactional database. The job record must never contain image
  bytes.
- **Retention:** treat deletion as a workflow across originals, derived images, caches,
  logs, and evaluation samples. Legal hold and user deletion must be explicit states.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="architecture" title="3. Design the architecture and model tiers" %}


### Keep durable orchestration outside the image model

{% system-flow title="Professional headshot job path" caption="The job controller owns durable state and policy evidence. Accelerator workers only perform bounded, retryable render stages." %}
{% system-node title="Admission" eyebrow="Protect" icon="shield" tone="rose" %}
Authenticate, reserve quota, record consent, scan uploads, and reject unsupported input before queueing work.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Identity package" eyebrow="Condition" icon="key" tone="blue" %}
Detect and align one face, derive a protected identity representation, and validate input diversity and quality.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Preview and final workers" eyebrow="Generate" icon="server" tone="violet" %}
Use a lower-cost preview tier for direction, then rerender the approved preset with a fixed model and seed manifest.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Release and delivery" eyebrow="Inspect" icon="check" tone="green" %}
Verify identity, professional composition, policy, and tenant preset before publishing immutable assets through a CDN.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}

### Assign each tier one job

- **Admission tier:** inexpensive classifiers and rules decide whether the upload,
  consent claim, and requested edit may proceed. It is fail closed and records a policy
  version with the job.
- **Conditioning tier:** produces face alignment, quality signals, and an identity
  representation scoped to the job. Raw reference images remain in protected object
  storage.
- **Preview tier:** uses fewer denoising steps and smaller output to validate framing,
  style, and user intent. A preview is never automatically promoted to final delivery.
- **Final tier:** uses the selected preset and versioned conditioning package to render
  the required resolution. It writes candidates to quarantine storage first.
- **Validation tier:** independently evaluates candidate identity similarity,
  composition, technical quality, policy, and brand adherence. It owns the release
  decision, not the generator.

### Make jobs retryable

Persist a job ID, immutable request manifest, consent receipt, chosen preset, model
versions, stage attempts, score reports, and final disposition. Queue messages contain
the job ID and stage only. A worker claims a stage with a lease, writes its candidate to
quarantine, and commits a stage transition conditionally. This prevents a duplicate
message from creating two externally visible final images.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="deep-dive" title="4. Deep dive into identity preservation and release safety" %}


### Identity is a release constraint, not a styling knob

The system may alter lighting, background, crop, and bounded attire categories. It must
not use a flattering score as proof that the generated face remains the authorized
person. Use multiple independent signals:

- **Input quality and diversity:** require a single visible face, sufficient resolution,
  pose variation, and clear lighting. Reject inputs that cannot support a faithful result.
- **Reference-to-output similarity:** compare protected embeddings from the accepted
  reference set to each candidate, calibrated by demographic slice and capture quality.
- **Edit boundary:** classify whether the candidate changes age cues, protected traits,
  apparent health, or professional credentials beyond the permitted preset.
- **Policy and provenance:** attach consent, source-asset IDs, model version, preset,
  score report, and disposition to every candidate.

### Exercise the release gate

{% interactive-block id="practice/headshot-generation-release-gate-lab" /%}

The threshold is not a universal constant. Calibrate it against false accepts and false
rejects across representative groups, then reserve human review for boundary cases.
Lowering the threshold to reduce rejections can turn a product-quality issue into an
identity and impersonation failure.

### Brand presets must remain bounded

An enterprise preset should be a reviewed configuration: palette family, background
class, crop range, lighting target, allowed attire categories, and model version. It is
not a prompt that can silently reshape someone's features. Store preset versions by
tenant, show the selected version in the audit trail, and re-evaluate approved presets
when models or policies change.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="evaluation" title="5. Define evaluation and quality assurance" %}


### Build a release decision from independent evidence

One aesthetic score cannot measure a professional, faithful, safe headshot. Evaluate a
versioned holdout set and production samples with separate signals:

{% concept-grid columns="3" %}
{% concept-card title="Faithfulness" eyebrow="Identity" icon="fingerprint" tone="blue" %}
Measure false accepts and false rejects for reference-to-output similarity, sliced by image quality and demographic group.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Professional quality" eyebrow="Photography" icon="scan-search" tone="violet" %}
Check face framing, eye placement, focus, lighting balance, artifacts, crop, and background appropriateness; use calibrated human review for disagreement.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Safe delivery" eyebrow="Policy" icon="shield-check" tone="green" %}
Track consent verification, blocked and reviewed cases, policy escapes, deletion completion, and tenant-preset violations.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

### Gate a model release

1. Freeze a representative evaluation set with consent, explicit retention rules, and
   documented coverage gaps.
2. Compare candidate and current models on identity errors, visual quality, policy
   precision and recall, queue time, failure rate, and cost per accepted final image.
3. Have trained reviewers inspect a stratified blind sample, especially low-confidence
   and high-impact cases.
4. Canary the model by tenant and preset, alert on score drift, and retain a fast
   rollback path to the prior approved model and threshold configuration.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Do not optimize only for acceptance rate or user preference. A model can look more
polished while becoming less faithful to the person in the reference photos.
{% /callout %}

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="governance" title="6. Control cost, privacy, and governance" %}


### Make the trade-offs explicit

- **Preview-first versus final-only:** previews reduce wasted final renders and give
  feedback, but create extra compute, storage, and a user decision boundary. Expire
  unselected previews aggressively.
- **Strict identity threshold versus throughput:** a strict threshold reduces false
  accepts but sends more jobs to review or retry. Treat review capacity as part of the
  product budget, not an afterthought.
- **Per-tenant fine-tuning versus preset conditioning:** fine-tuning may improve a
  narrow brand look but increases governance, evaluation, rollback, and leakage risk.
  Start with reviewed presets and isolated configuration.
- **Long retention versus user control:** retention helps reruns and dispute handling,
  but increases privacy exposure. Use explicit default expiry, delete workflows, and
  minimal audit evidence.

### Enforce governance at boundaries

- Encrypt source and output assets; restrict decryption to the job's tenant and purpose.
- Separate raw photos from derived embeddings, and define independent retention for both.
- Log human-review access and ensure review tools show only the minimum media needed.
- Version policies, thresholds, model packages, and brand presets so a released image is
  explainable later.
- Maintain a red-team suite for impersonation, consent evasion, biased quality failures,
  and policy bypass attempts before model or policy changes ship.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="operations" title="7. Scale, operate, and recover safely" %}


### Isolate queues by deadline and blast radius

Use separate queues for interactive previews, interactive finals, enterprise batch jobs,
and human review. Each job carries a deadline, tenant quota, model tier, and idempotency
key. Admission control should slow or defer low-priority work when queue age threatens
the interactive SLO, rather than letting all classes fail together.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Observe" %}
Track queue age, abandoned previews, accelerator throughput, GPU errors, stage retries, identity-score distribution, release rate, and deletion backlog by model and tenant.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Contain" %}
Pause a model or preset on drift, reduce low-priority admission, and quarantine candidates when a release dependency is unhealthy.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Recover" %}
Retry only idempotent stages with bounded attempts. Route ambiguous jobs to review or request new photos; never publish a partial candidate.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Learn" %}
Record the incident and score evidence, repair the evaluation suite, and canary a corrected model or policy before broad rollout.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}

### Define failures before launch

- **Worker loss:** reclaim the expired stage lease and retry from the durable job state;
  do not regenerate after a candidate has already passed release.
- **Identity service outage:** stop final release and keep completed candidates in
  quarantine. A lower-quality image is acceptable; an unverified likeness is not.
- **Queue overload:** return an honest ETA, preserve the job, prioritize paid
  interactive work according to policy, and defer batch jobs.
- **Model regression:** halt new jobs for the affected model/preset, roll back the
  versioned package, and re-review candidates generated during the affected window.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% accordion-item id="wrapup" title="8. Wrap up with risks and extensions" %}


### Defend the design in an interview

The central choice is to make a headshot a durable, gated job rather than a direct
model response. Admission protects consent and input quality; identity conditioning and
bounded presets guide generation; independent validation owns release; and queues keep
expensive work reliable under bursty demand. The system degrades by asking for better
inputs, extending an ETA, or routing to review, never by silently weakening safeguards.

### Risks that remain

- Identity embeddings and face-quality models can perform unevenly across capture
  conditions and demographic groups, so calibration and human evaluation must be
  ongoing.
- Consent and ownership cannot always be inferred from pixels. Product policy, user
  attestation, auditability, and escalation paths are part of the technical design.
- Enterprise brand pressure can become appearance manipulation. Keep presets bounded,
  reviewed, tenant-scoped, and easy to roll back.
- Cost can rise sharply when preview retries, rejected inputs, or GPU fragmentation
  increase. Monitor accepted-final cost, not only raw render cost.

### Sensible next increments

1. Start with consented, single-subject images, one professional preset family, and a
   conservative identity-and-policy release gate.
2. Add preview approval, enterprise preset review, queue isolation, and a trained
   human-review workflow after baseline reliability is proven.
3. Expand to more styles only after representative evaluation, appeal handling, and
   deletion/audit operations work at production volume.

{% /accordion-item %}

{% /accordion %}

